{
  "case_id": "2012-12-133",
  "document_hash": "6c9ca02ab89619ee4d04422844bbf2a100a82dda982232d048d801cba5a7355a",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Michael Kellogg",
      "case_id": "2012-12-133",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c9ca02ab89619ee4d04422844bbf2a100a82dda982232d048d801cba5a7355a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c9ca02ab896-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The court below thrice refused to enforce the parties' arbitration agreement because he thought that class procedures were necessary to vindicate the plaintiff's Sherman Act claims. That holding was reversible error for at least three reasons. First, it has no basis in either the FAA or the Sherman Act. Second, it creates an unworkable threshold inquiry. And third, it is unnecessary to any legitimate policy concerns raised by the court below."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2012-12-133",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c9ca02ab89619ee4d04422844bbf2a100a82dda982232d048d801cba5a7355a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c9ca02ab896-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Mr. Kellogg, suppose it goes to arbitration as you think it should, and the arbitrator says to the merchant, to prove your case, you have to show the relevant market, you have to show that American Express had market power, that it used that power to the detriment of its competitors, and the way"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2012-12-133",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c9ca02ab89619ee4d04422844bbf2a100a82dda982232d048d801cba5a7355a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c9ca02ab896-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: This is Case Number 12-133, American Express v. Italian Colors Restaurant."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Michael Kellogg",
      "case_id": "2012-12-133",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c9ca02ab89619ee4d04422844bbf2a100a82dda982232d048d801cba5a7355a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c9ca02ab896-0000-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KELLOGG: Well, three responses to that, Your Honor. The first is, that it is up to the arbitrator in the first instance to devise procedures to deal with claims in an efficient and cost-effective manner. Second, to the extent that an expert report is required that would cost a lot of money, we have conceded below that the parties could share costs of that expert just as they could share the costs of a lawyer."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2012-12-133",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "6c9ca02ab89619ee4d04422844bbf2a100a82dda982232d048d801cba5a7355a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c9ca02ab896-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I suppose that American Express wouldn't have had to agree to arbitration at all, right? They could have just said -- you know, you -- you have a cause of action, you sue us in court, right? They could say that, legally, couldn't they?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2012-12-133",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "6c9ca02ab89619ee4d04422844bbf2a100a82dda982232d048d801cba5a7355a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c9ca02ab896-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Our position is that multiple claimants in arbitration could share the costs of an expert for preparation of a report."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Plaintiffs",
      "case_id": "2012-12-133",
      "confidence": 0.93,
      "document_hash": "6c9ca02ab89619ee4d04422844bbf2a100a82dda982232d048d801cba5a7355a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c9ca02ab896-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "They said, we need the aggregate damages provided in a class action to make this worthwhile because if we're just going to essentially get costs --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2012-12-133",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "6c9ca02ab89619ee4d04422844bbf2a100a82dda982232d048d801cba5a7355a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c9ca02ab896-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But they could borrow the money from a lawyer instead of from the trade association, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2012-12-133",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "6c9ca02ab89619ee4d04422844bbf2a100a82dda982232d048d801cba5a7355a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c9ca02ab896-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Just to be sure I understand it, that you're saying that it does not violate the confidentiality agreement of this clause to -- to all get together and produce one report?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Paul D. Clement",
      "case_id": "2012-12-133",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "6c9ca02ab89619ee4d04422844bbf2a100a82dda982232d048d801cba5a7355a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c9ca02ab896-0001-0005",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF PAUL D. CLEMENT ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENTS"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2012-12-133",
      "confidence": 0.92,
      "document_hash": "6c9ca02ab89619ee4d04422844bbf2a100a82dda982232d048d801cba5a7355a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c9ca02ab896-0001-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "One other thing which I didn't understand, and that's why I am asking. What they chose as the remedy here was sever the arbitration clause if you want, it seemed to be, and go to court. All right. Now, I don't know where that power comes from."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Second Circuit",
      "case_id": "2012-12-133",
      "confidence": 0.96,
      "document_hash": "6c9ca02ab89619ee4d04422844bbf2a100a82dda982232d048d801cba5a7355a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c9ca02ab896-0001-0009",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It was the problem the Second Circuit saw."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2012-12-133",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "6c9ca02ab89619ee4d04422844bbf2a100a82dda982232d048d801cba5a7355a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c9ca02ab896-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Suppose this class could not -- could not qualify for certification in Federal court. Are you asserting that there is some arbitration principle that -- that allows you to create some new class?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2012-12-133",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "6c9ca02ab89619ee4d04422844bbf2a100a82dda982232d048d801cba5a7355a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c9ca02ab896-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: I'm not even talking about a pure exculpatory clause. I'm talking about the mere fact that as a practical matter, it's impossible to bring it in arbitration. In a context in which it is also impossible to bring it in Federal court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Kellogg",
      "case_id": "2012-12-133",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c9ca02ab89619ee4d04422844bbf2a100a82dda982232d048d801cba5a7355a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c9ca02ab896-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KELLOGG: Well, I -- I don't believe that's the case. The premise on which the Court accepted the case, presumably, is that the decision below which conditioned the enforceability of the arbitration agreement on a -- on the availability of class procedures, was wrong under Concepcion. Therefore -\u00ad"
    },
    {
